<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <!--
    THE BLAZOR FRONT END
    ====================
    A Blazor Web App running in InteractiveServer render mode, and a *client of the API* rather
    than a second head on the Application layer.

    Why an HTTP client instead of a project reference to LogiFlow.Application:

      * It is what you will be asked to build. A UI over an existing API is the common industrial
        shape - and it keeps the API the product, not an implementation detail of the web app.
      * It forces the contract to be real. This project redeclares the DTOs it consumes
        (see Contracts/) instead of sharing types, which is what any non-.NET consumer would have
        to do. If the API changes shape, this project should break - silently sharing a type
        would hide that.
      * The architecture tests stay meaningful. Nothing here can reach past the API into a
        handler or an entity, because it has no reference to them.

    Why InteractiveServer rather than WebAssembly:

      * The JWT never leaves the server. There is no token in browser storage, no CORS, and no
        refresh dance - the browser holds only a SignalR circuit. This is the same reasoning
        behind a backend-for-frontend, arrived at for free.
      * No multi-megabyte runtime download, which matters on a factory-floor tablet.
      * The trade-off, stated honestly: one stateful circuit per user. Cheap to start, expensive
        to scale, and the UI dies when the connection does. For an internal admin tool with tens
        of concurrent users - which is what most `gestionale` front ends are - that is the right
        trade. For a public site with thousands, it is not.

    Covered in: course/module-18-blazor/README.md
  -->

  <!--
    NO PACKAGE REFERENCES, AND ONE IN PARTICULAR THAT WAS DELIBERATELY NOT ADDED.

    Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Resilience is already in Directory.Packages.props, and
    .AddStandardResilienceHandler() on the typed client is a one-liner. It is not here because
    the standard handler retries failed requests, and this client's requests include:

        POST /api/orders            -> creates an order
        POST /api/orders/{id}/lines -> adds a line

    Neither is idempotent. A retry after a timeout - where the request actually SUCCEEDED and only
    the response was lost - creates a second order, or doubles a quantity. That is the exact
    failure the "POST, not PUT, to create" note in module 10 warns about, arriving through a
    library instead of a proxy.

    The correct fix is not "no resilience". It is retry on the idempotent reads only, plus an
    Idempotency-Key on the writes so a replay is safe - which is the endpoint-filter exercise at
    the end of module 15. Until that exists, retrying writes would be a bug wearing a best
    practice's clothing.
  -->

</Project>
